1
00:00:00,910 --> 00:00:09,850
Hello and welcome to this massive open online course introduction to bio risk management.

2
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:16,910
In this course, I will be teaching you about the different aspects of bio risk management.

3
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:26,690
Now, when we look at bio risk management, we think in terms of both biosafety and biosecurity.

4
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:32,390
And what exactly is the difference between biosafety and biosecurity?

5
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:33,890
I will give you an example.

6
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:42,470
For instance, biosafety management focuses on the unintentional release of a biological

7
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:45,270
agent.

8
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:50,710
For instance, if I am a researcher working in the lab, following all protocols, and there

9
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:56,710
is a mistake for what we call a breach of containment of that particular biological

10
00:00:56,710 --> 00:01:01,910
agent, this is an unintended release.

11
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:10,830
There is no prior motive for this release, and this falls in the domain of biosafety.

12
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:14,550
Now let us look at what biosecurity involves.

13
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:22,310
In biosecurity management, we don't look at the biological agent in terms of its biological

14
00:01:22,310 --> 00:01:23,310
activity.

15
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:30,310
Rather, we look at it in terms of its threat to the environment or to society.

16
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:39,030
So in this case, the biological agent is referred to as an asset or a biological asset.

17
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:46,710
And an asset can be used for nefarious purposes by organizations or individuals who may want

18
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:52,590
to use the biological asset as a threat.

19
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:58,270
So this falls in a different category in which there is a prior motive for the release of

20
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:02,230
that particular biological agent.

21
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:09,230
Now having clarified the concepts of biosafety and biosecurity, let us look at how these

22
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:12,670
threats can be addressed.

23
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:19,510
Both of these threats are addressed using a specific management strategy.

24
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:24,030
Now in all laboratory management strategies, we follow a cyclical approach.

25
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:29,910
So we have a plan, we have a do, we have a check, and we have an act.

26
00:02:29,910 --> 00:02:36,230
So it's called a PDCA, and it follows a cyclical procedure.

27
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:43,350
And in accordance with GLPs, or good laboratory practices, we always do what we write, and

28
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:45,030
we write what we do.

29
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:55,610
So these form the tenet of biosafety management, or in other words, good laboratory management.

30
00:02:55,610 --> 00:03:02,370
Now for the purpose of bioresc management, PDCA has been translated into what we call

31
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:07,010
as AMP.

32
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:12,170
So A refers to risk assessment.

33
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:16,610
M refers to risk mitigation.

34
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:21,770
And P refers to as performance assessment.

35
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:25,650
Let's look at this in a practical setting.

36
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:33,410
In order to work with any biological agent, be it a bacteria, a virus, a fungus, a genetically

37
00:03:33,410 --> 00:03:40,130
modified agent, whatever the case may be, we need to assess the risk posed by that

38
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:42,250
biological agent.

39
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:49,900
And this risk is measured in terms of likelihood and consequence.

40
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:57,620
Now when we do our risk assessment, we first identify the organism, or what we call the

41
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:07,400
biological agent, and we identify the risk associated with that biological agent.

42
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:15,160
For instance, if you are working with a virus, that is, spread via coughing and sneezing,

43
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:20,480
it implies that the virus can be aerosolized.

44
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:26,520
And if the aerosolized virus is infected to patients, the next step involves identifying

45
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:30,400
what exactly happens to that patient.

46
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,600
What is the root of infection, for instance, if the virus is aerosolized by sneezing and

47
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:39,920
the patient ingests the virus?

48
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:49,720
The next step will be asking yourself, is this biological agent going to cause morbidity,

49
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,880
which will lead to mortality?

50
00:04:52,880 --> 00:05:01,280
Assuming that the biological agent can cause morbidity, the next step will be to ask,

51
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:10,720
is a therapeutic measure available to address the issue of morbidity?

52
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:20,040
If it's going to cause mortality, you ask yourself again, do we have the pertinent measures to

53
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:24,240
limit mortality and morbidity?

54
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:34,360
Our risk is based on this entire gamut of information, which you can get when you work with a biological

55
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,840
agent.

56
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:43,480
To make it more comprehensive, the World Health Organization has divided biological agents

57
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,520
or classified them into risk groups.

58
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:52,360
We have risk group 1, risk group 2, risk group 3, and risk group 4, based on the precedence

59
00:05:52,360 --> 00:06:04,030
for the more higher level of lethality to human hosts.

60
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:12,190
Having said that, the process of handling the biological agent adds to the level of risk.

61
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:13,190
Let me give you an example.

62
00:06:13,190 --> 00:06:15,830
You are working with a virus in the laboratory.

63
00:06:15,830 --> 00:06:20,350
Now, when you are working with a virus in the laboratory, you may want to culture or

64
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:23,150
grow this virus.

65
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:28,550
Then you may want to centrifuge this virus to extract the genetic material, or you may

66
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:30,990
want to genetically modify this virus.

67
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:36,710
These are basically processes, and each process has a risk associated with that.

68
00:06:36,710 --> 00:06:41,350
Now, for example, if you culture the virus, you are increasing the copy number of the

69
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:42,350
virus.

70
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:47,270
When you increase the copy number, obviously, if there is a spill, you will have higher

71
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:50,270
consequences.

72
00:06:50,270 --> 00:06:54,790
When you centrifuge the virus, you may have aerosolization of the virus.

73
00:06:54,790 --> 00:07:02,230
This will add to the level of risk associated with that particular virus.

74
00:07:02,230 --> 00:07:09,030
What we address as biosafety managers is the risk associated with release of that biological

75
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:18,150
agent into the environment and its impact on laboratory workers, as well as the general

76
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:21,610
public.

77
00:07:21,690 --> 00:07:26,090
Okay, let us assume that you have a virus which is aerosolized.

78
00:07:26,090 --> 00:07:30,050
You need to contain that virus in a specific facility.

79
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:37,770
Now, I am speaking to you from a containment facility, which is a biosafety level 3 laboratory.

80
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:43,450
Biosafety level 3 laboratories basically contain the virus by using what is known

81
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:48,490
as an engineering control or directional airflow.

82
00:07:48,490 --> 00:07:54,410
In this facility, all the air is filtered in through EPA filters or high efficiency

83
00:07:54,410 --> 00:08:01,450
particulate filters, and the air exiting from this facility is filtered out through

84
00:08:01,450 --> 00:08:04,410
multiple EPA filters as well.

85
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:11,370
So, this constant flow of air ensures that the virus particles, if aerosolized, are

86
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:15,050
contained within the filter itself.

87
00:08:15,050 --> 00:08:20,650
This in turn protects the environment as well as the laboratory worker.

88
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:28,610
Okay, having said that, let us look at how risk is actually assessed.

89
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:35,490
Risk is assessed in terms of two factors, likelihood and consequence.

90
00:08:35,490 --> 00:08:44,090
Likelihood is basically a measure of how that particular biological agent can escape

91
00:08:44,090 --> 00:08:47,610
or breach containment in the laboratory.

92
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:56,170
For instance, if I am moving viruses around and the transport protocol is not very good,

93
00:08:56,170 --> 00:09:01,010
there may be a leak or a breach of containment of the virus during transport.

94
00:09:01,010 --> 00:09:07,210
So, if there is a breach of containment during transport, there will be consequences.

95
00:09:07,210 --> 00:09:14,490
These consequences can include exposure of the person carrying the virus to the biological

96
00:09:14,490 --> 00:09:22,250
agent and his or her subsequent mortality or morbidity.

97
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:28,210
So, these are factors which are critical to risk assessment.

98
00:09:28,210 --> 00:09:36,010
You assess based on likelihood of an accident as well as the consequences of that particular

99
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:37,610
accident.

100
00:09:37,610 --> 00:09:42,410
So, risk assessment is done in a structured manner.

101
00:09:42,410 --> 00:09:49,850
First, we go through the process, all the processes which are associated with the biological

102
00:09:49,850 --> 00:09:50,850
agent.

103
00:09:50,850 --> 00:09:55,890
And then we assess each process for the associated risk.

104
00:09:55,890 --> 00:10:00,370
And when this is done, we come out with a cumulative risk assessment.

105
00:10:01,370 --> 00:10:10,370
Now, when you have decided that your risk assessment is completed, you can then look at

106
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:12,410
the residual risk.

107
00:10:12,410 --> 00:10:20,130
What is the risk after you apply all the containment measures and is it viable or is

108
00:10:20,130 --> 00:10:23,850
it safe to work with that risk?

109
00:10:23,850 --> 00:10:29,170
That is what you look for in risk assessment.

110
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:33,010
We now move on to risk mitigation.

111
00:10:33,010 --> 00:10:41,010
Risk mitigation is basically the reduction of the risk posed by a biological agent by

112
00:10:41,010 --> 00:10:43,610
the application of pertinent controls.

113
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:49,290
We have five pertinent controls which you can remember in terms of the fingers of your

114
00:10:49,290 --> 00:10:50,290
hand.

115
00:10:50,290 --> 00:10:59,090
So, you have elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, and personal

116
00:10:59,090 --> 00:11:01,370
protective equipment.

117
00:11:01,370 --> 00:11:09,690
Five controls can be applied to address the risk posed by a biological agent.

118
00:11:09,690 --> 00:11:17,730
So, we mitigate the risk by A is elimination, in which case you decide that the experimental

119
00:11:17,730 --> 00:11:22,970
procedure is too risky and you do not conduct it at all.

120
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:24,490
That's elimination.

121
00:11:24,490 --> 00:11:26,650
The second one is substitution.

122
00:11:26,650 --> 00:11:33,690
You can attenuate or weaken a virus and work with it in an attenuated state or a weakened

123
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:34,690
state.

124
00:11:34,690 --> 00:11:37,970
The third one is engineering controls.

125
00:11:37,970 --> 00:11:41,170
For instance, now I am in a containment facility.

126
00:11:41,170 --> 00:11:45,610
This is an engineering control which has been designed by engineers to contain any

127
00:11:45,610 --> 00:11:48,410
biological agent within this facility.

128
00:11:48,410 --> 00:11:52,350
This is an engineering control.

129
00:11:52,350 --> 00:11:57,070
The next one, number four, is the administrative control.

130
00:11:57,070 --> 00:12:02,350
Administrative control is primarily concerned with standard operating procedures and adherence

131
00:12:02,350 --> 00:12:04,270
to these procedures.

132
00:12:04,270 --> 00:12:09,150
And finally, we have the fifth one is the personal protective equipment which I am

133
00:12:09,150 --> 00:12:10,150
wearing.

134
00:12:10,150 --> 00:12:15,110
Now, this is the last level at that hierarchy of controls.

135
00:12:15,110 --> 00:12:20,430
So, if all the other controls basically fail, I still have this personal protective

136
00:12:20,430 --> 00:12:27,150
equipment to protect me in the case of a breach of containment.

137
00:12:27,150 --> 00:12:35,150
Having said that, one has to understand that prior risk management is based on the judicious

138
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:42,830
use of these controls and they all work together with each other.

139
00:12:42,830 --> 00:12:50,190
For instance, you cannot have an engineering control without the requisite standard

140
00:12:50,190 --> 00:12:52,670
operating procedures.

141
00:12:52,670 --> 00:12:58,590
How do you decide the manner in which you enter and exit the facility?

142
00:12:58,590 --> 00:13:00,350
You need a procedure for this.

143
00:13:00,350 --> 00:13:08,840
So, this is listed in your biosafety manual as a standard operating procedure.

144
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:13,400
Your personal protective equipment.

145
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:19,280
This donning and doffing or wearing of this particular apparatus is also governed by

146
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:22,280
another standard operating procedure.

147
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:29,080
So, everything works in synchronicity with everything else.

148
00:13:29,080 --> 00:13:33,800
All the standard operating procedures, the engineering controls and personal protective

149
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:44,360
equipment together comprise the suite or the set of biosafety measures or by risk

150
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:52,600
management measures which we apply to mitigate the risk of exposure to the biological agent.

151
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:58,490
Okay, now we move on to performance.

152
00:13:58,490 --> 00:14:01,850
You have assessed the risk.

153
00:14:01,850 --> 00:14:11,770
You have applied the controls, but you want to identify any kind of mistakes in this process.

154
00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:16,210
This is where performance assessment comes into play.

155
00:14:16,210 --> 00:14:25,010
During performance assessment, an audit of the procedures is conducted and a bench audit

156
00:14:25,010 --> 00:14:33,930
can identify many of the procedures which may be cumbersome and which may pose a risk

157
00:14:33,930 --> 00:14:38,210
to the laboratory user or to the environment.

158
00:14:38,210 --> 00:14:40,930
Let us look at a basic procedure.

159
00:14:40,930 --> 00:14:45,930
For instance, I have written a standard operating procedure for donning and doffing.

160
00:14:45,930 --> 00:14:53,810
This particular PPE, but when the laboratory user enters the room for changing, he does

161
00:14:53,810 --> 00:14:56,650
not have the correct size of gloves.

162
00:14:56,650 --> 00:15:03,690
So, he uses a glove which is of a larger size or a smaller size.

163
00:15:03,690 --> 00:15:07,810
And when he enters the laboratory, the glove pairs.

164
00:15:07,810 --> 00:15:13,210
So, the laboratory worker reports this.

165
00:15:13,210 --> 00:15:18,890
He says that, oh, I went to the change room, but I did not have the glove of the requisite

166
00:15:18,890 --> 00:15:20,130
size.

167
00:15:20,130 --> 00:15:26,170
And I wore a glove which was smaller and it fell in the laboratory and there was a breach

168
00:15:26,170 --> 00:15:27,170
of containment.

169
00:15:27,170 --> 00:15:35,330
Now, these records of misses or near misses are very important in performance assessment.

170
00:15:35,330 --> 00:15:42,210
The biosafety officer plays a critical role in observing the behavior of the laboratory

171
00:15:42,210 --> 00:15:51,050
users and in identifying the possible reasons for the breach in containment.

172
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:56,450
Performance assessment adds to the entire AMP cycle.

173
00:15:56,450 --> 00:16:04,930
So, identification of faults during performance assessment can be applied to improve the procedures

174
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:11,530
and practices by a safety facility.

175
00:16:11,530 --> 00:16:20,450
This is basically an informal introduction to what we will be discussing during our MOOC.

176
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:30,030
I have tried to address certain issues like virus management in terms of AMP in order

177
00:16:30,030 --> 00:16:34,890
to give you an overview of the entire process.

178
00:16:34,890 --> 00:16:44,690
I hope that you can participate in our MOOC on Introduction to Biorisk Management as this

179
00:16:44,690 --> 00:16:57,250
will enable you to be prepared for any future pandemics which may arise due to the increase

180
00:16:57,250 --> 00:17:04,010
in the number of incidents and accidents at the global level.

181
00:17:04,010 --> 00:17:12,950
I have tried to incorporate elements in this MOOC which will be of pertinent to administrators

182
00:17:12,950 --> 00:17:21,770
to the biosafety officers, to the facility managers as well as to the laboratory users.

183
00:17:21,770 --> 00:17:27,170
Certain elements of this course can also be incorporated when you design your own facility

184
00:17:27,170 --> 00:17:33,910
such as the engineering controls as well as the directional controls.

185
00:17:33,910 --> 00:17:42,530
If you have any questions regarding this MOOC, please post these in the comments section below.

186
00:17:42,530 --> 00:17:51,670
I have developed a specific set of instructions which will be given to you as slides during

187
00:17:51,670 --> 00:17:52,670
this MOOC.

188
00:17:52,670 --> 00:17:57,350
Thank you very much for watching this video.

189
00:17:57,350 --> 00:18:06,950
I wish you a very pleasant learning experience and above all, stay biosafe.

190
00:18:06,950 --> 00:18:07,870
Thank you very much.
